## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of low-level synchronization, reusable barriers, and memory ordering with atomics.

## Key Concepts
- C11 atomics and memory_order semantics
- Sense-reversing barrier pattern
- Reusability and performance on multi-core CPUs

## Code Example
```javascript
// skeleton in C-like pseudocode
typedef struct {
  _Atomic int count;
  int total;
  _Atomic int sense;
} barrier_t;

void barrier_init(barrier_t *b, int n){ b->count = 0; b->total = n; atomic_store(&b->sense, 0); }

void barrier_wait(barrier_t *b, int *local_sense){
  int my = *local_sense;
  int arrived = atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&b->count, 1, memory_order_acq_rel);
  if (arrived == b->total - 1){
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->count, 0, memory_order_relaxed);
    *local_sense = 1 - my;
    atomic_store_explicit(&b->sense, 1 - atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_relaxed), memory_order_release);
  } else {
    while (atomic_load_explicit(&b->sense, memory_order_acquire) == my) { /* spin */ }
  }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt for dynamic thread counts?
- How would NUMA-aware placements affect performance?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical low-level data structure design under bounded resources, including probing, memory layout, and edge handling (empty slots, full table).

## Key Concepts
- Open addressing with linear probing
- Fixed capacity and sentinel handling
- Separate arrays for keys and values for cache locality
- Return conventions for success/failure

## Code Example
```c
#include <stdint.h>
#include <stddef.h>

#define N (1<<12)
static uint32_t keys[N];
static uint64_t vals[N];

static inline size_t probe(uint32_t k) { return k % N; }

void ht_init() {
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) keys[i] = 0;
}

int ht_insert(uint32_t k, uint64_t v) {
  if (k == 0) return -1; // sentinel reserved
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) { keys[idx] = k; vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    if (keys[idx] == k) { vals[idx] = v; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1; // full
}

int ht_lookup(uint32_t k, uint64_t* out) {
  if (k == 0) return -1;
  size_t idx = probe(k);
  for (size_t i = 0; i < N; ++i) {
    if (keys[idx] == 0) return -1;
    if (keys[idx] == k) { *out = vals[idx]; return 0; }
    idx = (idx + 1) % N;
  }
  return -1;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you resize or rehash when load factor grows? 
- How to detect and recover from clustering effects in probing? 
- What are alternatives to sentinel-based empties (bitmaps, tombstones)?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of safe, non-blocking reclamation in a shared object graph, balancing hazard pointers with epoch-based techniques. The scenario emphasizes NUMA locality and cross-thread safety, common in database-like systems.

## Key Concepts

- Hazard pointers
- Epoch-based reclamation
- Per-thread fast paths
- Memory ordering and fences
- NUMA considerations

## Code Example

```c
typedef struct Obj {
  atomic_uint refcount;
  atomic_uint epoch;
  struct Obj* next;
} Obj;

void pin(Obj* o) { atomic_fetch_add_explicit(&o->refcount, 1, memory_order_acquire); }
void unpin(Obj* o) { if (atomic_fetch_sub_explicit(&o->refcount, 1, memory_order_release) == 1) retire(o); }

void retire(Obj* o) { add_to_retire_list(o, current_thread); if (should_advance_epoch()) scan_and_free(o); }
void* get(Obj* o) { pin(o); /* access */ }
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle long-lived objects and cycles with epoch-based reclamation?
- What are the edge cases when a thread exits or stalls while objects are pinned?

